#version 460

#ifdef USE_RT
/* [QL] E161: the ray-traced variants (r_rtDlightShadows) - SPIR-V 1.4 */
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : require
#endif

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform UBO {
	// light/env parameters:
	vec4 eyePos;				// vertex
	vec4 lightPos;				// vertex: light origin
	vec4 lightColor;			// fragment: rgb + 1/(r*r)
	vec4 lightVector;			// fragment: linear dynamic light
#ifdef USE_FOG
	// fog parameters:
	vec4 fogDistanceVector;		// vertex
	vec4 fogDepthVector;		// vertex
	vec4 fogEyeT;				// vertex
	vec4 fogColor;				// fragment
#endif
#ifdef USE_RT
	/*
	[QL] E161. After the fog block at fixed offsets, so the layout matches
	vkUniform_t whether or not this variant declares fog.

	  rtOrigin, rtAxis0..2  the draw's object-to-world transform (the entity's
	                        orientation, or identity for the world)
	  rtParams              x strength 0..1, y rays per pixel, z light size in
	                        world units (soft edge), w how far short of the
	                        light the ray stops; rtLight.w 1 for the debug
	                        tint (r_rtDlightShadows 2)
	*/
	layout(offset = 128) vec4 rtOrigin;
	layout(offset = 144) vec4 rtAxis0;
	layout(offset = 160) vec4 rtAxis1;
	layout(offset = 176) vec4 rtAxis2;
	layout(offset = 192) vec4 rtLight;
	layout(offset = 208) vec4 rtParams;
#endif
};

#ifdef USE_RT
/* the level's acceleration structure - the static one, see vk_rt_update_main_descriptor */
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform accelerationStructureEXT topLevel;
#endif

layout(set = 1, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D texture0;
#ifdef USE_FOG
layout(set = 2, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D fogtexture;
#endif

/*
[QL] R20 Stage A: the lighting stage's normal map, derived from its own diffuse
texture's luminance at shader-parse time (R_DeriveNormalMap), or
tr.flatNormalImage when the surface has none. It is always bound: a flat image
decodes to (0,0,1) in tangent space and perturbs by nothing, so a surface
without a map lights exactly as it did.

This sampler was here once before and was removed. The reason is below, it is
still the reason, and the two things it names - IMGFLAG_NOLIGHTSCALE on every
normal image, and art that actually carries maps - are the two conditions Stage
A had to meet before putting it back.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The whole design rested on one claim: bind a flat normal when a stage has none,
because a flat normal decodes to (0,0,1), the cotangent frame maps that straight
back to the interpolated normal, and a surface without a map therefore lights
exactly as it did. The claim was right. The premise was not - the image was not
flat by the time the shader read it.

tr.flatNormalImage is (128,128,255), and it was created without
IMGFLAG_NOLIGHTSCALE, so the upload path ran R_LightScaleTexture over it like
any other colour texture. That table is built with the overbright shift folded
in - inf <<= tr.overbrightBits - so at the default r_overBrightBits 1, the 128s
came out as 255. The "flat" normal decoded to (1,1,1), normalized to a 55 degree
tilt in tangent space, applied to every dynamically lit fragment in the game.

A constant tangent-space tilt is not a constant world-space one. Tangent space
is built from the surface's texture axes, so the tilt pointed a different way on
every face, and the symptom was slits of light across large surfaces whose angle
changed from room to room with the mapper's texture alignment - north-south in
one, east-west in the next. Every dynamic light did it: rocket in flight, rocket
impact, muzzle flash, plasma.

Not fixed, removed. The flag is a one-word fix, but the count this code prints
at the end of registration has been 0 on every map: Quake Live's shaders carry
the normalMap keywords and ship no normal map images, so there is nothing for
the perturbation to do but cost a fetch and be wrong. It goes back when there is
art that needs it - and when it does, both the flat image and every real normal
map need IMGFLAG_NOLIGHTSCALE, because a normal map is data and this path
gamma-corrects and overbrights anything that does not say otherwise.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both conditions are met now. tr.flatNormalImage carries IMGFLAG_NOLIGHTSCALE and
so does every map R_DeriveNormalMap creates, and "ships no normal map images" is
answered by deriving them from the diffuse rather than by waiting for art. The
count printed at the end of registration reports the shipped maps and the
derived ones separately, so the shipped number staying at 0 stays visible.
*/
layout(set = 3, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D normalmap;

layout(location = 0) centroid in vec2 frag_tex_coord;
layout(location = 1) in vec3 N;  // normalized object-space normal vector
layout(location = 2) in vec4 L;  // object-space light vector
layout(location = 3) in vec4 V;  // object-space view vector
#ifdef USE_FOG
layout(location = 4) in vec2 fog_tex_coord;
#endif
layout(location = 5) in vec3 P;      // [QL] R20 Stage A: object-space position
layout(location = 6) in vec2 tc_lin; // [QL] R20 Stage A: non-centroid texcoord, derivatives only

layout(location = 0) out vec4 out_color;

layout (constant_id = 0) const int alpha_test_func = 0;
layout (constant_id = 1) const float alpha_test_value = 0.0;
//layout (constant_id = 2) const float depth_fragment = 0.85;
layout (constant_id = 3) const int alpha_to_coverage = 0;
//layout (constant_id = 4) const int color_mode = 0;
layout (constant_id = 5) const int abs_light = 0;
/*
[QL] E104. The green-channel convention arrives in eyePos.w, not as a
specialization constant.

These pipelines are built once at init, so a spec constant here could only ever
carry the GLOBAL default - and the convention is a property of the material, so
a third-party map declaring qlNormalFlipG needs it per draw. eyePos.w is free:
the vertex shader computes V = eyePos - vec4(position, 1.0) and nothing reads
V.w. The C side puts the global cvar XORed with the stage's own declaration
there.

The bump pass does use a spec constant for the same value, because its pipeline
is built per draw and can afford to.
*/

#ifdef USE_RT
vec3 rtToWorld(vec3 v) {
	return rtAxis0.xyz * v.x + rtAxis1.xyz * v.y + rtAxis2.xyz * v.z;
}

float rtHash(vec2 p, float n) {
	return fract(sin(dot(p, vec2(12.9898, 78.233)) + n * 37.719) * 43758.5453);
}

/*
[QL] E161: how much of the light reaches this fragment, 0..1.

One ray per sample from the surface to the light, against the level. A point
light is one point; with a size (rtParams.z) the samples spread over a sphere
that big around it and the shadow edge softens by as much. The ray stops
rtParams.w short of the light so the geometry the light sits against - a
rocket touching a wall, a muzzle flash in a doorway - does not shadow it.
*/
float rtLightVisibility(vec3 Pobj, vec3 Nobj, vec3 Lobj) {
	vec3 wP = rtOrigin.xyz + rtToWorld(Pobj);
	vec3 wL = wP + rtToWorld(Lobj);
	vec3 wN = normalize(rtToWorld(Nobj));
	if (dot(wN, wL - wP) < 0.0) {
		wN = -wN;                   // two-sided surfaces: leave from the lit side
	}
	vec3 O = wP + wN * 1.0;

	int samples = clamp(int(rtParams.y), 1, 8);
	float occluded = 0.0;
	for (int i = 0; i < samples; i++) {
		vec3 target = wL;
		if (rtParams.z > 0.0) {
			vec3 r = vec3(rtHash(gl_FragCoord.xy, float(i)), rtHash(gl_FragCoord.yx, float(i) + 3.1),
			              rtHash(gl_FragCoord.xy * 1.7, float(i) + 7.3)) * 2.0 - 1.0;
			target += r * rtParams.z;
		}
		vec3 d = target - O;
		float dist = length(d);
		float tmax = dist - rtParams.w;
		if (tmax <= 1.0) {
			continue;               // close enough that nothing fits between
		}
		d /= dist;

		rayQueryEXT rq;
		rayQueryInitializeEXT(rq, topLevel,
			gl_RayFlagsTerminateOnFirstHitEXT | gl_RayFlagsOpaqueEXT,
			0xFF, O, 0.0, d, tmax);
		while (rayQueryProceedEXT(rq)) {}
		if (rayQueryGetIntersectionTypeEXT(rq, true) != gl_RayQueryCommittedIntersectionNoneEXT) {
			occluded += 1.0;
		}
	}
	return 1.0 - occluded / float(samples);
}
#endif

float CorrectAlpha(float threshold, float alpha, vec2 tc)
{
	ivec2 ts = textureSize(texture0, 0);
	float dx = max(abs(dFdx(tc.x * float(ts.x))), 0.001);
	float dy = max(abs(dFdy(tc.y * float(ts.y))), 0.001);
	float dxy = max(dx, dy); // apply the smallest boost
	float scale = max(1.0 / dxy, 1.0);
	float ac = threshold + (alpha - threshold) * scale;
	return ac;
}

void main() {
#ifdef USE_FOG
	vec4 fog = texture(fogtexture, fog_tex_coord);
#endif
	vec4 base = texture(texture0, frag_tex_coord);

	if ( alpha_to_coverage != 0 ) {
		if (alpha_test_func == 1) {
			base.a =  base.a > 0.0 ? 1.0 : 0.0;
		} else if (alpha_test_func == 2) {
			base.a = CorrectAlpha(alpha_test_value, 1.0 - base.a, frag_tex_coord);
		} else if (alpha_test_func == 3) {
			base.a = CorrectAlpha(alpha_test_value, base.a, frag_tex_coord);
		}
	} else
	// specialization: alpha-test function
	if (alpha_test_func == 1) {
		if (base.a == alpha_test_value) discard;
	} else if (alpha_test_func == 2) {
		if (base.a >= alpha_test_value) discard;
	} else if (alpha_test_func == 3) {
		if (base.a < alpha_test_value) discard;
	}

	vec4 lightColorRadius = lightColor;

#ifdef USE_LINE
	// project fragment on light vector
	float scale = clamp( dot( -L.xyz, lightVector.xyz ) * lightVector.w, 0.0, 1.0 );
	vec4 LL = lightVector * scale + L;

	vec3 nL = normalize(LL.xyz); // normalized light vector
	vec3 nV = normalize(V.xyz);	// normalized view vector

	// light intensity
	float intensFactor = 1.0 - (dot(LL.xyz, LL.xyz) * lightColorRadius.w);
#else
	vec3 nL = normalize(L.xyz);	// normalized light vector
	vec3 nV = normalize(V.xyz);	// normalized view vector

	// light intensity
	float intensFactor = 1.0 - dot(L.xyz, L.xyz) * lightColorRadius.w;
#endif

	if (intensFactor <= 0.0)
		discard;
	vec3 intens = lightColorRadius.rgb * intensFactor;

#ifdef USE_FOG
	// modulate base by inverted fog alpha
	base.xyz = base.xyz * ( 1.0 - fog.a );
#endif

	/*
	[QL] R20 Stage A: perturb the interpolated normal by the bound normal map,
	in a tangent basis built from screen-space derivatives. Q3 BSP vertices
	carry no tangents and adding them would change the vertex format, so the
	basis is solved per fragment from how P and the texture coords move across
	the pixel quad.

	Cramer's rule on the 2x2 UV Jacobian. The two relations the quad gives are
	dPdx = T*duv1.x + B*duv1.y and dPdy = T*duv2.x + B*duv2.y; solving for T
	component-wise gives ( duv2.y*dPdx - duv1.y*dPdy ) / det. duv1.y, not
	duv2.x: the two agree on axis-aligned UVs and the wrong one mirrors the
	tangent on rotated UVs and skews it on sheared ones, which Quake maps use
	constantly, so an axis-aligned test wall proves nothing about it.

	det keeps its sign through the floor, so a near-degenerate Jacobian flips
	handedness rather than dividing by zero, and a face with no usable tangent
	at all falls back to an arbitrary one off the geometric normal instead of
	emitting NaN.

	With tr.flatNormalImage bound this reduces to normalize(N) to within the
	rounding of its bytes - 0.318 degrees, measured, not assumed.
	*/
	vec3 nN;
	{
		vec3 geomN = normalize(N);

		vec3 dPdx = dFdx(P);
		vec3 dPdy = dFdy(P);
		vec2 duv1 = dFdx(tc_lin);
		vec2 duv2 = dFdy(tc_lin);

		float a01 = duv1.x * duv2.y;
		float b01 = duv2.x * duv1.y;
		float det = ((a01 >= b01) ? 1.0 : -1.0) * max(abs(a01 - b01), 1e-6);

		vec3 Traw = (duv2.y * dPdx - duv1.y * dPdy) / det;
		if (dot(Traw, Traw) < 1e-8) {
			vec3 up = (abs(geomN.y) < 0.99) ? vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0) : vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0);
			Traw = cross(up, geomN);
		}

		vec3 Tn = normalize(Traw - geomN * dot(geomN, Traw));
		vec3 Bn = cross(geomN, Tn);

		vec3 tsn = normalize(texture(normalmap, frag_tex_coord).xyz * 2.0 - 1.0);
		if (eyePos.w != 0.0) {
			tsn.y = -tsn.y;
		}

		nN = normalize(Tn * tsn.x + Bn * tsn.y + geomN * tsn.z);
	}

	// Lambertian diffuse reflection term (N.L)
	float diffuse = dot(nN, nL);

	// specular reflection term (N.H)
	float specFactor = dot(nN, normalize(nL + nV));

	if ( abs_light != 0 )
	{
		// make sure that light and eye vectors are on the same plane side
		if ( diffuse * dot(nN, nV) <= 0 )
			discard;

		diffuse = abs( diffuse );
		specFactor = abs( specFactor );
	}

	/*
	[QL] A surface facing away from the light receives no light. It does not
	receive negative light.

	dot(N,L) is negative for every fragment on the far side of a surface from
	the light, and nothing clamped it. This pass blends ONE/ONE, so a negative
	fragment colour is subtracted from the scene - the dynamic light darkens the
	side of the geometry it does not reach, by as much as it brightens the side
	it does.

	It was invisible for as long as the scene target was fixed-point, because
	Vulkan clamps the source and destination of a blend to [0,1] before
	evaluating it when the attachment is fixed-point, and does not clamp at all
	when it is floating-point. r_rts supplies a floating-point target. That is
	the whole of why this appeared with r_rts and only with r_rts, and why
	r_rts 2 did not help: B10G11R11 is floating-point too, so it is equally
	unclamped at blend time and only clamps when the result is written - which
	stops the pixel going below black without stopping it going below what was
	already there.

	Reported as a grenade under a walkway lighting the underside and putting a
	black circle on the top, the circle shrinking with the light's radius. That
	is exactly this: intensFactor bounds the circle at the radius, and the sign
	of dot(N,L) picks which face of the walkway is lit and which is subtracted
	from.

	pow() of a negative base is undefined in GLSL, and specFactor went straight
	into pow(specFactor, 10.0), so the specular term had the same problem with
	less of a pattern to it.

	After the abs_light branch, not before: that mode deliberately takes the
	absolute value of both to light two-sided surfaces from either face, and
	max(abs(x), 0.0) is abs(x), so one clamp here is correct for both paths.
	*/
	diffuse = max( diffuse, 0.0 );
	specFactor = max( specFactor, 0.0 );

#ifdef USE_RT
	/* [QL] E161: the level stands between this fragment and the light */
	if (rtParams.x > 0.0 && (diffuse > 0.0 || specFactor > 0.0)) {
#ifdef USE_LINE
		vec3 Lobj = LL.xyz;
#else
		vec3 Lobj = L.xyz;
#endif
		float vis = rtLightVisibility(P, N, Lobj);
		float lost = clamp(rtParams.x, 0.0, 1.0) * (1.0 - vis);
		if (rtLight.w > 0.0) {
			/* [QL] E162 debug (r_rtDlightShadows 2): the blocked light turns
			   red instead of going away, so it is obvious where it acts */
			float i0 = max(max(intens.r, intens.g), intens.b);
			intens = intens * (1.0 - lost) + vec3(i0 * lost, 0.0, 0.0);
		} else {
			intens *= 1.0 - lost;
		}
	}
#endif

	//float spec = pow(specFactor, 8.0) * 0.25;
	vec4 spec = vec4(pow(specFactor, 10.0)*0.25) * base * 0.8;

	out_color = (base * vec4(diffuse) + spec) * vec4(intens, 1.0);
}
